{"findings":[{"category":"validation","description":"createUser casts body.age to number without checking it. Negative, fractional, or non-numeric ages are stored and returned, although docs/api.md requires a whole number from 0 to 150 and the README requires 400 VALIDATION_FAILED on bad input. requireNonNegativeInteger in src/lib/validate.ts already implements the check.","id":"F1","severity":"high","sourceRefs":[{"endLine":34,"relPath":"src/handlers/user.ts","startLine":34},{"endLine":41,"relPath":"src/lib/validate.ts","startLine":29}],"title":"age is not validated on user creation"},{"category":"error_handling","description":"When the order store throws, listOrders answers 200 with an empty body. This breaks the public response format ({ data } | { error }) and hides the failure. It should call sendError so the client receives 500 INTERNAL.","id":"F2","severity":"high","sourceRefs":[{"endLine":57,"relPath":"src/handlers/orders.ts","startLine":51},{"endLine":48,"relPath":"src/errors.ts","startLine":40}],"title":"listOrders swallows store errors and returns an empty 200"},{"category":"correctness","description":"elapsed() divides by 1000, so /health reports uptimeMs in seconds. Its doc comment and docs/api.md both say milliseconds.","id":"F3","severity":"medium","sourceRefs":[{"endLine":10,"relPath":"src/lib/format.ts","startLine":3},{"endLine":15,"relPath":"src/handlers/health.ts","startLine":8}],"title":"elapsed() returns seconds but is documented as milliseconds"}],"outcome":"findings","sourceRefs":[{"endLine":34,"relPath":"src/handlers/user.ts","startLine":34},{"endLine":57,"relPath":"src/handlers/orders.ts","startLine":51},{"endLine":10,"relPath":"src/lib/format.ts","startLine":3}],"summary":"Three defects found: POST /users stores age without validation, GET /orders hides store failures behind an empty 200 response, and elapsed() returns seconds although it is documented to return milliseconds.","unresolvedQuestions":[]}